r years since  britain voted to leave the eu  that vote divided a nation now the  pandemic is the focus and as crystal  commansing explains  the health crisis has given the country  a new sense of optimism  at first glance it's hard to figure out  what's happening at this tiny shop  it's a music store but there are fresh  baguettes  and a friendly hello will get you an  offer of food  the store has become a hive of activity  kindly donated as the staging ground for  charitable operation mobilizing  volunteers  and food to feed those in need it's the  most incredible feeling  jenny mathias is energy in motion  ping-ponging from talking to packing to  getting crates of food loaded into cars  and onto bikes it's like christmas it's  like every day  we are we put ourselves in the position  to be hopefully father christmas to  everybody every day  the pandemic didn't stop food for all  the charity like many got busier but  there's also been a surge in desire to  help  at a time when people are struggling  sick and dying  brits are displaying their gratitude and  getting involved  69 percent of adults say britain will be  kind after the pandemic  the office of national statistics survey  found before kovitt 19  only 39 percent would say that and  56 percent thought post virus it would  be united  a jump from 19 percent it's one of the  most  unifying experiences i think i've seen  like in this country  especially coming out of an extremely  rocky period by that lauren spielman  means  brexit remember all of those emotionally  charged rallies and debates  i am currently on furlough as many  people are  and i am trying to do something  meaningful  with my fellow time this is the year of  compassion  this covid19 thing may be a bad thing  but it's also  it's also given us all everybody  involved in it which is everybody around  the world  a chance to observe their compassion  a bit of humanity when we all need it  the most  crystal gomansing global news london  well put that